/-!
# The component branch of the residual Thompson argument

The current exports place each C-component of J(DR₁) in D, prove its
noncentral action on E, and identify the original point stabilizer inside
the literal quotient action. That stabilizer has trivial two-residual and
contains a Sylow subgroup of the component.

The remaining source argument must construct the B.5.1 decomposition with
the actual orthogonal A₅ module or at most two isomorphic L₃(2) modules,
then prove that its marked point stabilizer has nontrivial two-residual.
The definitions in Components.MarkedActions specify these module markings;
they do not assert that the original action has them. Component exclusion
and hence solvability of J are not yet established here.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, combined manuscript of 1 March 2004,
14.7.67(4), printed p.1070, PDF p.1087.
-/
